      "inputTokens": 83,
      "outputTokens": 598,
      "costUsd": 0.0054234999999999995,
      "pronunciationText": "Why do so many curves fit? Adding a constant slides a curve straight up or down, without tilting it anywhere. At every x, the slid curve has the same gradient as x squared, because the derivative of a constant is zero. So if capital F is one primitive, F plus any constant is also a primitive. We write the constant as C, and the family as F of x, plus C."
    },
