      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 115,
      "outputTokens": 887,
      "costUsd": 0.0080405,
      "pronunciationText": "But could a curve that isn't a slid copy also have gradient two x? Take any two primitives of the same function, and look at the gap between them. For two of the shoots, it stays thirty centimetres all the way up. Their gradients are equal at every x, so the gap's gradient is zero everywhere. A function whose gradient is zero everywhere never rises and never falls. So over an unbroken interval, it stays constant. That means any two primitives differ by a constant, and F of x, plus C, covers every one of them."
    },
