      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 158,
      "outputTokens": 1260,
      "costUsd": 0.011418999999999999,
      "pronunciationText": "So how do we find one primitive? Reverse the power rule. Differentiating multiplies by the power, then lowers it by one. To undo it, raise the power by one, then divide by the new power. Try x squared. Raising the power gives x cubed. But x cubed differentiates to three x squared, three times too big. So divide by three. x cubed over three differentiates to x squared. Watch its gradient. At every x, the gradient of x cubed over three equals the height of x squared. In general, x to the n plus one, over n plus one, differentiates to x to the n. The n plus one cancels, as long as it isn't zero. So for x above zero, it works for every real n except minus one."
    },
